package ferrum:plugin@1.0.0;

// CAPABILITY. Grant name: "usage-read".
// Consent: "see how many tokens your AI coding tools have used".

/// How much an AI coding tool on this machine has been used, in tokens.
///
/// The numbers exist already: tools like Claude Code record the token counts of
/// every model call in their own session files, on disk, belonging to the user.
/// A plugin that wanted to show "how much have I used today" could read those
/// files directly if it had the authority to — and that is precisely what makes
/// this interface worth having, because that authority is enormous and this one
/// is not.
///
/// **The host reads, the plugin gets numbers.** A session file is a verbatim
/// transcript: every prompt, every reply, every path, every project name. To
/// draw one percentage a plugin would otherwise read all of it. So the host
/// does the reading and hands across counts, and the transcript never crosses
/// the ABI. The consent line can then honestly say "how many tokens", because
/// that is the whole of what is on offer.
///
/// This is the same shape as `cap-net-fetch`'s credential: the sensitive thing
/// stays host-side and the guest is given the one derived fact it needs.
///
/// # Ages, not timestamps
///
/// There is no wall clock in this world — see `cap-clock`, which says why: it
/// is a fingerprinting and correlation surface for no benefit. Buckets are
/// therefore labelled by how long ago they started, not by when they were.
///
/// That is not a workaround, it is the better shape for the question. "How much
/// in the last five hours" is a rolling window, and a rolling window is
/// naturally expressed in ages. A guest that knew the wall time could correlate
/// this machine's activity with anything else it ever sees; a guest that knows
/// "42,000 tokens between three and four hours ago" can draw exactly the same
/// reading and nothing else.
///
/// # What this is not
///
/// It is not billing, not a quota, and not a subscription balance. It reports
/// what was *recorded locally* by a tool on this machine. A host that had those
/// other numbers would have got them from a service, and getting them from a
/// service is `cap-net-fetch` with a credential — a different decision, made
/// separately.
interface usage-read {
  use types.{host-error};

  /// Which tool's records to read.
  ///
  /// A closed enum rather than a string, so a guest cannot name a path or a
  /// process and have the host go looking. Adding a tool is a host change and
  /// a new consent wording, which is the correct amount of friction for
  /// "somewhere else on your disk the editor will now read".
  enum tool {
    claude-code,
  }

  /// One hour of recorded use.
  ///
  /// Hourly because it is the coarsest bucket that still answers every question
  /// a reading asks — a five-hour window, a day, a week — and because a week of
  /// them is 168 records rather than the twenty-five thousand model calls they
  /// summarise. A guest wanting a total adds them up; a guest wanting a chart
  /// has one bar each.
  record bucket {
    /// How many whole hours before now this bucket started. `0` is the hour in
    /// progress, `1` the one before it.
    ///
    /// The hour in progress is partial, and a guest showing it as a completed
    /// figure will draw a number that climbs all hour and then resets. That is
    /// the guest's problem to present, not the host's to hide.
    hours-ago: u32,

    /// Tokens sent, excluding anything served from cache.
    input-tokens: u64,
    /// Tokens generated.
    output-tokens: u64,
    /// Tokens written into a prompt cache. Billed differently by every service
    /// that offers it, which is why it is not folded into `input-tokens`.
    cache-write-tokens: u64,
    /// Tokens read back from a prompt cache. Usually the largest number here by
    /// two orders of magnitude, and usually the cheapest.
    cache-read-tokens: u64,
    /// How many model calls this bucket summarises.
    calls: u64,
  }

  /// Hourly buckets covering the last `hours`, most recent first.
  ///
  /// Hours with no recorded use are omitted rather than returned as zeroes: a
  /// week of mostly-idle buckets is mostly padding, and a guest summing a list
  /// gets the same answer either way.
  ///
  /// The host bounds `hours` and may return fewer buckets than asked for. It
  /// reads files off a disk, and a guest asking for ten years of history should
  /// get a refusal or a truncation rather than a frozen window.
  ///
  /// Fails when the tool is not installed, when its records cannot be read, and
  /// when the host has no implementation wired up. All three are `host-error`
  /// rather than an empty list, because "nothing recorded" and "could not look"
  /// are different answers and a guest showing 0% for the second is lying.
  buckets: func(recorded-by: tool, hours: u32) -> result<list<bucket>, host-error>;
}
